     "mdText": "[HEADER] unit 1 — CD1\n\n[1] Conversation — track 1, 3, 4\nHazel runs into her old classmate Owen at a farmers market.\nHazel: Owen? No way! I **haven't seen** you **since** graduation!\nOwen: Hazel! Wow, it's been forever. How have you been?\nHazel: Great, thanks! I'm a nurse now. Are you still in Denver?\nOwen: No, I moved to Seattle. **I've been** there **for** three years.\nHazel: Seattle? Nice! So what brings you back to town?\nOwen: My sister's getting married on Saturday. **I've been** in town **since** Monday.\nHazel: Oh, how exciting! Tell her congratulations from me.\nOwen: I will. Hey, let's grab coffee before I fly back!\n\n[2] Speak Like a Native — track 2\n1. How have you been?\nweak: “have”, “you”\nstrong: “How”, “been”\n“have” đọc rất nhẹ, gần như chỉ còn âm /v/ dính vào “How”: “haov-yơ-bin”. Giọng Mỹ đọc “been” là /bɪn/ “bin”, ngắn, không kéo dài.\n\n2. I haven't seen you since graduation!\nchange: “haven't seen” (mất âm t)\nstrong: “haven't”, “graduation”\nÂm /t/ cuối của “haven't” gần như mất trước “seen”: “he-vơn-sin”. “graduation” nhấn vào âm “-a-” thứ hai: “gre-ju-ÊI-shần”.\n\n3. I've been in town since Monday.\nweak: “I've been”\nlinking: “been‿in”\nstrong: “town”, “Monday”\n“I've” đọc /aɪv/; âm /v/ rất nhẹ nhưng đừng bỏ, vì “I been” là sai ngữ pháp. “been in” nối thành “bi-nin”.\n\n[MẸO PHÁT ÂM]\n“have / has” làm trợ động từ thường đọc nhẹ /həv/, /həz/, hoặc rút gọn: I've, she's.\nGiọng Mỹ đọc “been” là /bɪn/ “bin”, kể cả khi được nhấn.\nÂm /t/ cuối của “haven't, hasn't” hay mất trước phụ âm: haven't seen → “he-vơn-sin”.\n“for” đọc nhẹ /fər/; nhấn vào phần thời gian phía sau: for three YEARS, since MONday.\n\n[3] Study Notes\n**It's been forever.**\nLâu lắm rồi mới gặp lại!\nCâu cảm thán khi gặp lại ai đó sau nhiều năm. “forever” ở đây là cách nói phóng đại, nghĩa là “rất, rất lâu”.\nCách nói tương đương: It's been ages!\n\n**What brings you back to town?**\nCó việc gì mà bạn về lại đây thế?\n“What brings you (here / to…)?” là cách hỏi thân thiện, lịch sự về lý do ai đó đến một nơi.\nCách nói tương đương: What are you doing back in town?\n\n[4] Grammar Focus\nHiện tại hoàn thành: have / has + V3\nNói về việc bắt đầu trong quá khứ và vẫn còn đến bây giờ. Phủ định: haven't / hasn't + V3. Khi nói thường rút gọn: I've, we've, she's.\n[[I've known]] Lan since elementary school.\nMy dad [[has worked]] at the bank for twenty years.\nShe [[hasn't called]] me for two weeks.\n\nfor / since và câu hỏi How long…?\nfor + một khoảng thời gian (for two days, for ages); since + mốc bắt đầu (since Monday, since I was ten). Hỏi độ dài: How long have you…?\nI've had this laptop [[for]] five years.\nHe's been sick [[since]] Friday.\n[[How long]] have you lived here? – [[Since]] last spring.\n\n[5] Role Play — track 6\nA: Owen? No way! I haven't seen you since graduation!\nB: Hazel! Wow, it's been forever. How have you been?\nA: Great, thanks! I'm a nurse now. Are you still in Denver?\nB: No, I moved to Seattle. I've been there ________.\nA: Seattle? Nice! So what brings you back to town?\nB: My sister's getting married on Saturday. I've been in town ________.\nA: Oh, how exciting! Tell her congratulations from me.\nB: I will. Hey, let's ________ before I fly back!\n\n[6] Substitution Drill — track 7\n1. I've been there __for three years__.\na. for about a year\nb. since last summer\nc. since I changed jobs\n\n2. I've been in town __since Monday__.\na. since Thursday night\nb. for a few days\nc. for almost a week\n\n3. Hey, let's __grab coffee__ before I fly back!\na. have lunch\nb. get dinner\nc. catch up properly\n",
