     "mdText": "[HEADER] unit 10 — CD3\n\n[1] Conversation — track 8, 10, 11\nIt's seven o'clock on Friday evening. Jerome and Priya are still at the office, and they have a big pitch on Monday.\nJerome: Right, I don't know about you, but I'm ready to **call it a day**.\nPriya: Same here. I've been **burning the midnight oil** all week.\nJerome: You do look a bit **under the weather**, actually. Are you all right?\nPriya: Just tired. It's been **one of those weeks**, you know?\nJerome: Too right. Before we go, are we **on the same page** about Monday?\nPriya: I think so. You **break the ice**, and I'll handle the numbers.\nJerome: Perfect. Now go home and **put your feet up**. That's an order!\nPriya: You don't have to tell me twice. Have a great weekend!\n\n[2] Speak Like a Native — track 9\n1. Right, I don't know about you, but I'm ready to call it a day.\nchange: “don't know” (mất âm t)\nlinking: “call‿it‿a‿day”\nstrong: “ready”, “day”\nNgắt ba cụm: “Right,” / “I don't know about you,” / “but I'm ready to call it a day”. “call it a day” nối thành “co-li-tơ-đây” (giọng Anh giữ /t/).\n\n2. I've been burning the midnight oil all week.\nweak: “I've been”\nlinking: “midnight‿oil‿all”\nstrong: “midnight oil”, “week”\n“I've been” đọc nhanh, nhẹ: “aiv-bin”. Giọng Mỹ: t → d nhẹ, cả cụm nối thành “mít-nai-đoi-lol”.\n\n3. It's been one of those weeks, you know?\nlinking: “one‿of”\nweak: “of”\nstrong: “those”\n“one of” nối thành “wa-nơv”. Nhấn “those”: “những tuần như thế” (ai cũng hiểu là tệ). “you know?” lên giọng nhẹ, mời người nghe đồng cảm.\n\n[MẸO PHÁT ÂM]\nThành ngữ đọc liền như một từ dài: call it a day → “co-li-tơ-đây” (giọng Mỹ: “co-li-đơ-đây”).\nCâu dài chia thành cụm ngắn theo ý, ngừng nhẹ ở dấu phẩy.\nTrong “one of those weeks / days”, nhấn “those” để người nghe tự hiểu là chẳng có gì suôn sẻ.\n\n[3] Study Notes\n**You break the ice, and I'll handle the numbers.**\nAnh mở màn cho không khí thoải mái, còn em lo phần số liệu.\n“break the ice” là phá băng: nói vài câu mở đầu để mọi người bớt ngại. “handle” là phụ trách, xử lý.\nCách nói tương đương: You start, and I'll do the numbers.\n\n**You don't have to tell me twice.**\nKhông cần nhắc lần hai đâu (mình về ngay đây)!\nĐáp lại vui vẻ khi ai bảo mình làm một việc mà mình đang rất muốn làm.\nCách nói tương đương: Say no more!\n\n[4] Grammar Focus\nThành ngữ về công việc: call it a day / burn the midnight oil / on the same page\ncall it a day = nghỉ, dừng làm việc; burn the midnight oil = thức khuya làm việc, học bài; be on the same page = hiểu và thống nhất với nhau.\nWe're all exhausted. Let's [[call it a day]].\nShe's [[burning the midnight oil]] before her exams.\nLet's meet first so we're [[on the same page]].\n\nThành ngữ về sức khoẻ, nghỉ ngơi: under the weather / put your feet up\nfeel under the weather = hơi mệt, khó ở; put your feet up = ngồi nghỉ thư giãn; one of those days / weeks = ngày, tuần chẳng có gì suôn sẻ.\nI'm feeling a bit [[under the weather]] today.\nAfter work, I just want to [[put my feet up]].\nNothing's going right. It's [[one of those days]].\n\n[5] Role Play — track 13\nA: Right, I don't know about you, but I'm ready to call it a day.\nB: Same here. I've been ________ all week.\nA: You do look a bit under the weather, actually. Are you all right?\nB: Just tired. It's been ________, you know?\nA: Too right. Before we go, are we on the same page about Monday?\nB: I think so. You ________, and I'll handle the numbers.\nA: Perfect. Now go home and put your feet up. That's an order!\nB: You don't have to tell me twice. Have a great weekend!\n\n[6] Substitution Drill — track 14\n1. I've been __burning the midnight oil__ all week.\na. snowed under\nb. running on empty\nc. working flat out\n\n2. It's been __one of those weeks__, you know?\na. a long week\nb. a crazy few days\nc. nonstop since Monday\n\n3. You __break the ice__, and I'll handle the numbers.\na. kick things off\nb. do the intro\nc. warm them up\n",
