     "md": "Level 9/unit 2/unit 2.md",
     "mdText": "[HEADER] unit 2 — CD1\n\n[1] Conversation — track 8, 10, 11\nBarry asks Beatrice about the mountain hike she did on Saturday.\nBarry: **Judging by** that sunburn, I reckon you made it to the top.\nBeatrice: Barely! **Having never hiked** that high before, I was totally unprepared.\nBarry: Unprepared? You're the fittest person I know.\nBeatrice: Fitness wasn't the problem. I set off before dawn **wearing** just a hoodie.\nBarry: A hoodie? Up a mountain? Oh, mate.\nBeatrice: It gets worse. **Caught** in a downpour halfway up, we nearly turned back.\nBarry: But you kept going, obviously.\nBeatrice: Yep. **Exhausted** and starving, we reached the summit just as the clouds cleared.\nBarry: Oh, so that's where the sunburn came from. Worth it, though?\nBeatrice: Totally. **Looking back**, I'd do it again. Just not in a hoodie.\n\n[2] Speak Like a Native — track 9\n1. A hoodie? Up a mountain? Oh, mate.\nstrong: “hoodie”, “mountain”\nlinking: “Up‿a”\nHỏi lại vì không tin nổi: lên giọng mạnh ở cuối mỗi câu hỏi. “Up a” nối thành “ấp-pơ”. Giọng Úc hay đọc “mate” gần như “mai(t)”.\n\n2. Caught in a downpour halfway up, we nearly turned back.\nlinking: “Caught‿in‿a”\nstrong: “downpour”, “nearly”, “back”\nNgắt nhẹ sau mệnh đề phân từ (sau “halfway up”). Giọng Mỹ: t cuối từ gặp nguyên âm thành d nhẹ, “Caught in a” → “ca-đi-nơ”.\n\n3. Looking back, I'd do it again.\nstrong: “Looking back”, “again”\nlinking: “do‿it‿again”\n“Looking back” là một cụm riêng: hơi lên giọng ở cuối rồi ngắt nhẹ. “do it again” nối liền, t thành d nhẹ: “đu-i-đơ-ghen”.\n\n[MẸO PHÁT ÂM]\nMệnh đề phân từ đầu câu là một cụm riêng: đọc liền một hơi, hơi lên giọng ở cuối, rồi ngắt nhẹ.\nCâu hỏi lặp lại (A hoodie?) lên giọng thật mạnh để tỏ ý ngạc nhiên, không tin.\nGiọng Úc (Barry): âm /eɪ/ thường nghe gần /aɪ/: mate → “mai(t)”, made → “mai(d)”.\n\n[3] Study Notes\n**It gets worse.**\nCòn tệ hơn nữa cơ.\nDùng khi kể chuyện, báo trước rằng phần sắp kể còn tệ hơn phần vừa kể.\nCách nói tương đương: That's not the worst of it.\n\n**Worth it, though?**\nNhưng có đáng không?\nCâu hỏi rút gọn của “Was it worth it?”; “though” cuối câu nghĩa là “nhưng mà”.\nCách nói tương đương: Was it worth all that?\n\n[4] Grammar Focus\nMệnh đề phân từ chủ động: V-ing / Having + V3\nRút gọn mệnh đề khi hai vế cùng chủ ngữ: V-ing cho việc xảy ra cùng lúc hoặc lý do, Having + V3 cho việc xảy ra trước.\n[[Walking]] to work, I bumped into my old teacher.\n[[Having finished]] the report, she went straight home.\n[[Having never driven]] abroad, he was pretty nervous.\n\nMệnh đề phân từ bị động (V3) và cụm cố định\nV3 đầu câu mang nghĩa bị động hoặc chỉ trạng thái (Caught in…, Exhausted…). Văn nói hay dùng các cụm cố định Judging by…, Looking back…\n[[Exhausted]], we fell asleep on the train.\n[[Judging by]] the crowd, this place must be good.\n[[Looking back]], I should've studied harder.\n\n[5] Role Play — track 13\nA: Judging by that sunburn, I reckon you made it to the top.\nB: Barely! Having never ________ before, I was totally unprepared.\nA: Unprepared? You're the fittest person I know.\nB: Fitness wasn't the problem. I set off before dawn wearing just ________.\nA: A hoodie? Up a mountain? Oh, mate.\nB: It gets worse. Caught in a downpour halfway up, we nearly turned back.\nA: But you kept going, obviously.\nB: Yep. ________, we reached the summit just as the clouds cleared.\nA: Oh, so that's where the sunburn came from. Worth it, though?\nB: Totally. Looking back, I'd do it again. Just not in a hoodie.\n\n[6] Substitution Drill — track 14\n1. Having never __hiked that high__ before, I was totally unprepared.\na. camped outdoors\nb. climbed a mountain\nc. carried a heavy pack\n\n2. I set off before dawn wearing just __a hoodie__.\na. a T-shirt\nb. shorts and sneakers\nc. a thin jacket\n\n3. __Exhausted and starving__, we reached the summit just as the clouds cleared.\na. Soaked and shivering\nb. Tired but happy\nc. Aching all over\n",
