     "mdText": "[HEADER] unit 3 — CD1\n\n[1] Conversation — track 15, 17, 18\nDanielle and her partner Cyrus rent a tiny flat in Sydney.\nDanielle: Don't laugh, but what if we moved to the country?\nCyrus: **Granted**, this place is tiny. But we'd be in the middle of nowhere.\nDanielle: We both work from home, though. And the rent's half what we pay.\nCyrus: **Admittedly**, that's tempting. **Even so**, what would we do all weekend?\nDanielle: Bushwalks, the beach, farmers' markets. You'd love it, I reckon.\nCyrus: I'd miss the food, though. Where would I get ramen at midnight?\nDanielle: Fair point. **That said**, when did you last eat ramen at midnight?\nCyrus: Okay, you got me there. **Still**, I'm a city guy at heart.\nDanielle: Then let's do a trial run. Just a month.\nCyrus: Fine. But the minute I see a snake, I'm out.\n\n[2] Speak Like a Native — track 16\n1. Admittedly, that's tempting.\nchange: “Admittedly” (t → d nhẹ)\nstrong: “Admittedly”, “tempting”\nGiọng Mỹ đọc “ơd-mí-đơd-li”. Ngắt nhẹ sau “Admittedly”, rồi xuống và hơi lên giọng ở “tempting”: còn ý “nhưng” phía sau.\n\n2. That said, when did you last eat ramen at midnight?\nchange: “did‿you” (d + y → “j”)\nlinking: “last‿eat”\nstrong: “last”, “midnight”\nGiọng Úc thường đọc “last” với âm a dài /lɐːst/ như giọng Anh, khác giọng Mỹ /læst/. Nhấn “last” để trêu: lâu lắm rồi còn gì.\n\n3. Fine. But the minute I see a snake, I'm out.\nlinking: “see‿a”\nweak: “the”, “a”\nstrong: “Fine”, “snake”, “out”\n“Fine.” xuống giọng, kéo dài: đồng ý miễn cưỡng. “see a” nối bằng âm “y”: “si-yơ”. Nhấn “snake”, “out”, giọng nửa đùa nửa thật.\n\n[MẸO PHÁT ÂM]\nGiọng Mỹ: t giữa hai nguyên âm thành “d” nhẹ (Admittedly); t sau n hay bị nuốt khi nói nhanh (Granted → “gren-nịd”).\nGiọng Úc và Anh đọc a dài trong last, after, can't; giọng Mỹ đọc âm /æ/ bẹt hơn.\nSau Granted, Admittedly, That said: ngắt nhẹ để báo hiệu ý ngược lại sắp đến.\nKhi trêu hay phản bác, dồn trọng âm vào từ chốt: last, midnight, snake.\n\n[3] Study Notes\n**But we'd be in the middle of nowhere.**\nNhưng mình sẽ ở chỗ khỉ ho cò gáy.\n“in the middle of nowhere” = nơi hẻo lánh, xa mọi thứ. Người Anh, Úc còn nói “miles from anywhere”.\nCách nói tương đương: But we'd be out in the sticks.\n\n**But the minute I see a snake, I'm out.**\nNhưng hễ thấy rắn là anh chuồn ngay.\n“the minute + mệnh đề” = ngay khi (thân mật hơn “as soon as”); “I'm out” = tôi rút, không chơi nữa.\nCách nói tương đương: As soon as I see a snake, I'm gone.\n\n[4] Grammar Focus\nGranted, … / Admittedly, …: nhượng bộ trước khi phản bác\nGranted: công nhận ý của người kia; Admittedly: thừa nhận một điều bất lợi cho chính mình. Sau đó thường là “But…”.\n[[Granted]], it's pricey. But it'll last for years.\n[[Admittedly]], I've never actually been there.\n\nThat said, … / Even so, …: đưa ra ý ngược lại\nĐứng đầu câu, sau khi đã nhượng bộ một ý, để nêu ý ngược lại; nghĩa gần “dù vậy”. Still dùng tương tự.\nThe hotel was noisy. [[That said]], the staff were lovely.\nIt might rain. [[Even so]], we're going ahead.\nI know it's late. [[Still]], I'd like to finish this.\n\n[5] Role Play — track 20\nA: Don't laugh, but what if we moved to the country?\nB: Granted, ________. But we'd be in the middle of nowhere.\nA: We both work from home, though. And the rent's half what we pay.\nB: ________, that's tempting. Even so, what would we do all weekend?\nA: Bushwalks, the beach, farmers' markets. You'd love it, I reckon.\nB: I'd miss the food, though. Where would I get ramen at midnight?\nA: Fair point. That said, when did you last eat ramen at midnight?\nB: Okay, you got me there. Still, I'm ________ at heart.\nA: Then let's do a trial run. Just a month.\nB: Fine. But the minute I see a snake, I'm out.\n\n[6] Substitution Drill — track 21\n1. Granted, __this place is tiny__.\na. the rent's insane\nb. the traffic's awful\nc. we need more space\n\n2. __Admittedly__, that's tempting.\na. To be fair\nb. I'll admit\nc. Granted\n\n3. Still, I'm __a city guy__ at heart.\na. a city slicker\nb. a New Yorker\nc. a night owl\n",
