     "mdText": "[HEADER] unit 10 — CD3\n\n[1] Conversation — track 8, 10, 11\nJulian has cooked dinner for his friend Lydia.\nJulian: Right, dinner is served. My famous lasagne. You're welcome.\nLydia: Famous? Is that why the smoke alarm's been going off all afternoon?\nJulian: That's a smoky finish, **I'll have you know**. Very fancy.\nLydia: **Says the man who** burnt toast. Twice. In one morning.\nJulian: Ah, that's **rich, coming from** someone who can't boil an egg.\nLydia: Excuse me, my eggs are a delicacy. A crunchy delicacy.\nJulian: Go on, have a taste. And be brutally honest.\nLydia: Okay, fine. It's actually not bad. **Don't let it go to your head**.\nJulian: Too late. I'm already pitching my own cooking show.\nLydia: Lovely. But maybe **don't give up the day job**, yeah?\n\n[2] Speak Like a Native — track 9\n1. Right, dinner is served. My famous lasagne.\nlinking: “dinner‿is”\nstrong: “served”, “famous”\nGiọng Ireland thường đọc /eɪ/ gần “ê”: “famous” → “fê-mơs”, và đọc rõ /r/ (dinner, served). “lasagne” đọc “lơ-zan-yơ”. Nhấn “famous”, giọng tự hào cố tình phóng đại.\n\n2. Says the man who burnt toast.\nchange: “burnt toast” (hai âm t nhập làm một)\nstrong: “man”, “burnt toast”\n“Says” đọc /sez/ “xez”, không phải “xêiz”. Giọng Anh không đọc /r/ trong “burnt”: “bơnt”. Ngắt ngắn sau câu này để câu đùa “ngấm”.\n\n3. But maybe don't give up the day job, yeah?\nchange: “don't give” (mất âm t)\nlinking: “give‿up”\nstrong: “day job”, “yeah”\n“give up” nối thành “gi-vắp”. “yeah?” cuối câu lên giọng, giống “nhé?”, cho thấy đây là lời trêu chứ không phải chê thật.\n\n[MẸO PHÁT ÂM]\nGiọng Ireland thường đọc rõ /r/ (dinner, served) và đọc /eɪ/ gần “ê” (famous, taste); giọng Anh thì không.\n“says” đọc /sez/, “said” đọc /sed/, khác hẳn “say” /seɪ/.\nTrêu đùa cần nhịp: ngắt ngắn trước câu chốt (Twice. In one morning.) rồi nói chậm câu chốt.\nGiọng giả vờ nghiêm trọng hay tự ái (I'll have you know) báo hiệu là đang đùa.\n\n[3] Study Notes\n**My famous lasagne. You're welcome.**\nMón lasagne trứ danh của tôi đây. Khỏi cảm ơn.\nNói “You're welcome” trước khi được cảm ơn là kiểu khoe vui, cố tình tự tin quá mức.\nCách nói tương đương: Thank me later.\n\n**That's a smoky finish, I'll have you know.**\nCho cô biết nhé, đó là vị khói đấy.\n“I'll have you know” = cho bạn biết (nghe trịnh trọng), hay dùng khi giả vờ tự ái để bênh mình.\nCách nói tương đương: For your information, it's meant to be smoky.\n\n[4] Grammar Focus\nĐáp trả khi bị trêu: Says the man who… / That's rich, coming from…\nDùng khi người kia chê mình nhưng chính họ cũng chẳng hơn gì (kiểu “nói ai chứ…”). Giọng vui, không gay gắt.\nLazy? [[Says the woman who]] slept till noon!\n[[That's rich, coming from]] you!\n\nTrêu mà không làm mất lòng\nKhen kèm chút trêu (đừng có mà kiêu), hoặc chê khéo một tài lẻ (đừng bỏ nghề chính nhé). Chỉ dùng với người thân quen.\nNice goal. [[Don't let it go to your head]].\nGreat singing! [[Don't give up the day job]], though.\nRelax, [[I'm only pulling your leg]].\n\n[5] Role Play — track 13\nA: Right, dinner is served. My famous lasagne. You're welcome.\nB: Famous? Is that why the smoke alarm's been going off all afternoon?\nA: That's a smoky finish, I'll have you know. Very fancy.\nB: Says the man who ________. Twice. In one morning.\nA: Ah, that's rich, coming from someone who can't boil an egg.\nB: Excuse me, my eggs are a delicacy. A crunchy delicacy.\nA: Go on, have a taste. And be brutally honest.\nB: Okay, fine. It's actually ________. Don't let it go to your head.\nA: Too late. I'm already pitching my own cooking show.\nB: Lovely. But maybe don't ________, yeah?\n\n[6] Substitution Drill — track 14\n1. Says the man who __burnt toast__.\na. set the microwave on fire\nb. puts ketchup on pasta\nc. can't make a cup of tea\n\n2. It's actually __not bad__.\na. pretty decent\nb. surprisingly good\nc. really tasty\n\n3. But maybe don't __give up the day job__, yeah?\na. hand in your notice\nb. get too big for your boots\nc. invite any food critics\n",
