     "mdText": "[HEADER] unit 12 — CD3\n\n[1] Conversation — track 22, 24, 25\nMiranda, from Chicago, has just started work in Sydney. Nathan is her new colleague.\nMiranda: Okay, translate for me. My boss said she'd see me this arvo. Should I be worried?\nNathan: Relax, it just means afternoon. Speaking of which, **barbie** at mine Sunday **arvo**?\nMiranda: I'd love to! Should I bring anything?\nNathan: Just bring a plate. There'll be **heaps** of food.\nMiranda: A plate? You guys don't own dishes?\nNathan: No, a plate of food! A salad or something. And wear your **thongs**.\nMiranda: Excuse me? Back home, thongs are underwear!\nNathan: Flip-flops! I meant flip-flops! Okay, you'll need a **fortnight** to get the hang of this.\nMiranda: A fortnight? You sound like my British grandma.\nNathan: Two weeks, then. See? You're learning heaps already.\n\n[2] Speak Like a Native — track 23\n1. Speaking of which, barbie at mine Sunday arvo?\nlinking: “Speaking‿of”\nstrong: “barbie”, “Sunday arvo”\nGiọng Úc không đọc /r/: “barbie” → “ba-bi”, “arvo” → “a-vâu”. Câu hỏi rút gọn (bỏ “Do you want to come to a…”), lên giọng ở cuối.\n\n2. Excuse me? Back home, thongs are underwear!\nlinking: “thongs‿are”\nstrong: “Excuse me”, “underwear”\nLên giọng thật cao ở “Excuse me?” vì sốc. Giọng Mỹ đọc rõ /r/: “underwear” → “ăn-đơr-wer”.\n\n3. Okay, you'll need a fortnight to get the hang of this.\nchange: “get the” (t gần như mất)\nlinking: “hang‿of”\nstrong: “fortnight”, “hang”\n“fortnight” = hai tuần, giọng Úc đọc “fo-t-nait”, không có /r/. “hang of this” đọc liền: “hang-ơv-đhis”.\n\n[MẸO PHÁT ÂM]\nTừ rút gọn đuôi -ie, -o nhấn âm đầu: BREK-kie, BAR-bie, AR-vo.\nGiọng Úc không đọc /r/ cuối âm tiết (arvo, barbie, fortnight); giọng Mỹ đọc rõ (underwear).\nGiọng Úc thường đọc /eɪ/ gần “ai”: plate → “plai-t”, mate → “mai-t”.\nCâu hỏi lặp lại lời người kia (A plate? A fortnight?) lên giọng ở cuối để tỏ ý ngạc nhiên.\n\n[3] Study Notes\n**Just bring a plate.**\nCứ mang theo một đĩa đồ ăn là được.\nỞ Úc, “bring a plate” = mang một món ăn đến tiệc để mọi người cùng ăn, không phải mang đĩa không!\nCách nói tương đương: Bring something to share.\n\n**You'll need a fortnight to get the hang of this.**\nBạn sẽ cần hai tuần mới quen được đấy.\n“fortnight” (Anh, Úc) = hai tuần; người Mỹ gần như không dùng. “get the hang of something” = quen tay, nắm được cách làm.\nCách nói tương đương: It'll take you two weeks to get used to it.\n\n[4] Grammar Focus\nTừ rút gọn kiểu Úc: thêm -ie / -o\nNgười Úc hay cắt ngắn từ rồi thêm -ie hoặc -o: afternoon → arvo, breakfast → brekkie, barbecue → barbie, sunglasses → sunnies.\nLet's grab [[brekkie]] at the beach.\nSee you this [[arvo]]!\nDon't forget your [[sunnies]].\n\nCùng nghĩa, khác từ: Úc / Anh / Mỹ\nheaps (Úc) = loads (Anh) = a ton (Mỹ): rất nhiều. fortnight (Anh, Úc) = two weeks. thongs (Úc) = flip-flops (Mỹ, Anh).\nWe've got [[heaps]] of time.\nI'm off for a [[fortnight]] in June.\nGrab your [[thongs]]. We're off to the beach.\n\n[5] Role Play — track 27\nA: Okay, translate for me. My boss said she'd see me this arvo. Should I be worried?\nB: Relax, it just means afternoon. Speaking of which, barbie at mine ________?\nA: I'd love to! Should I bring anything?\nB: Just bring a plate. There'll be heaps of food.\nA: A plate? You guys don't own dishes?\nB: No, a plate of food! A salad or something. And wear ________.\nA: Excuse me? Back home, thongs are underwear!\nB: Flip-flops! I meant flip-flops! Okay, you'll need a fortnight to get the hang of this.\nA: A fortnight? You sound like my British grandma.\nB: Two weeks, then. See? You're learning ________ already.\n\n[6] Substitution Drill — track 28\n1. Speaking of which, barbie at mine __Sunday arvo__?\na. this arvo\nb. after work\nc. on the weekend\n\n2. And wear __your thongs__.\na. your sunnies\nb. a hat\nc. sunscreen\n\n3. You're learning __heaps__ already.\na. loads\nb. a ton\nc. so much\n",
